WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.282
For the next three weeks, Anne and Priscilla continued to feel like strangers in a strange land.

2
00:00:06.283 --> 00:00:14.582
But as they attended classes and social events, and became familiar with the professors and other students, Anne and Priscilla felt better.

3
00:00:14.583 --> 00:00:19.433
The first-year students, instead of being a collection of unrelated individuals,

4
00:00:19.434 --> 00:00:27.458
became close acquaintances who all attended class events and shared a class song and class spirit.

5
00:00:27.459 --> 00:00:36.840
Gilbert Blythe was elected class president. He was also invited to join a fraternity, which was a rare honor for a first-year student.

6
00:00:36.841 --> 00:00:39.307
As an initiation to the fraternity,

7
00:00:39.308 --> 00:00:47.038
Gilbert had to walk down the main streets of Halifax for a whole day wearing a bonnet and a colorful kitchen apron.

8
00:00:47.039 --> 00:00:51.676
He did this cheerfully, shaking ladies' hands as they passed.

9
00:00:51.677 --> 00:00:57.109
Anne and Priscilla found themselves very involved in the social life of Redmond College.

10
00:00:57.110 --> 00:01:01.091
Philippa Gordon came from a rich and well-known family.

11
00:01:01.092 --> 00:01:06.581
This, along with her beauty and charm, got her invited to many parties and events.

12
00:01:06.582 --> 00:01:10.765
And wherever she went, Anne and Priscilla went too.

13
00:01:10.766 --> 00:01:18.620
It was a mystery to Anne how Phil found time to study because she was always going out, especially with young men.

14
00:01:18.621 --> 00:01:24.197
Nine-tenths of the male students in first year were rivals for Phil's smiles.

15
00:01:24.198 --> 00:01:33.256
"Your two special hometown 'friends', Alec and Alonzo, don't seem to have any serious rivals yet," remarked Anne teasingly. "Not

16
00:01:33.257 --> 00:01:39.036
one," agreed Philippa. "Of course, I can't get the boy I like best. Gilbert

17
00:01:39.037 --> 00:01:44.900
Blythe won't take any notice of me, except to look at me as if I'm a little kitten he'd like to pat. I

18
00:01:44.901 --> 00:01:50.304
know the reason, Anne. It's clear that Gilbert likes you .

19
00:01:50.305 --> 00:01:54.332
I really ought to hate you, but instead I adore you!

20
00:01:54.333 --> 00:01:57.915
Around you, I wish I was wiser and stronger.

21
00:01:57.916 --> 00:02:01.519
Anne, please tell me over again that you like me a little bit!"

22
00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:10.096
"I like you a lot! And I think you're a dear, sweet, adorable little . . . kitten ." Anne

23
00:02:10.097 --> 00:02:15.186
laughed. "But I don't understand how you ever have time to

24
00:02:15.187 --> 00:02:23.539
study!" Somehow Phil must have found time to study because she was the top student in every class except English, where Anne came first by

25
00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:29.312
far. Anne found her studies easy, leaving her time for a social life that she thoroughly

26
00:02:29.313 --> 00:02:36.370
enjoyed. But never for a moment did she forget Avonlea and her family and friends

27
00:02:36.371 --> 00:02:40.877
there. For Anne, the happiest time of each week was when she read letters from

28
00:02:40.878 --> 00:02:50.548
home. The first batch had contained letters from Ruby Gillis, Diana, Marilla, Mrs. Lynde, and Jane Andrews, who had taken over as the new

29
00:02:50.549 --> 00:02:55.189
schoolteacher. Jane's letter was neatly written, without an interesting sentence in

30
00:02:55.190 --> 00:03:00.527
it. She didn't answer any of the questions that Anne had asked in her letter to

31
00:03:00.528 --> 00:03:07.196
Jane. But Jane did tell Anne how many yards of lace she had crocheted and how she felt when her head

32
00:03:07.197 --> 00:03:13.224
ached. Ruby Gillis wrote about how much she was missing Anne and asked what the Redmond boys were

33
00:03:13.225 --> 00:03:19.704
like. Ruby's letter was silly and harmless, and Anne would have laughed except for one

34
00:03:19.705 --> 00:03:24.603
part. "Gilbert seems to be enjoying Redmond, judging from his letters," wrote

35
00:03:24.604 --> 00:03:27.614
Ruby. "I don't think Charlie likes it so

36
00:03:27.615 --> 00:03:37.398
much." So Gilbert was writing to Ruby! "Gilbert can write to anybody he wants to, of course," thought Anne, "but . .

37
00:03:37.399 --> 00:03:40.985
." Anne was completely confused by her

38
00:03:40.986 --> 00:03:48.392
feelings. What Anne didn't know was that Ruby had written to Gilbert first and that he had simply answered to be

39
00:03:48.393 --> 00:03:52.900
polite. Marilla's letter was rather prim, with no gossip or

40
00:03:52.901 --> 00:03:58.039
emotion. Yet somehow it conveyed the wholesome, simple life at Green

41
00:03:58.040 --> 00:04:01.840
Gables. Mrs. Lynde's letter was full of church

42
00:04:01.841 --> 00:04:08.884
news. "I wonder," said Anne as she folded up her letters, "what Mrs. Lynde would think of

43
00:04:08.885 --> 00:04:17.093
Philippa." One fine winter day, Gilbert, Anne, Priscilla, Charlie, and Phil met to go for a walk through

44
00:04:17.094 --> 00:04:20.791
Halifax. "Let's go down Spofford Avenue," suggested

45
00:04:20.792 --> 00:04:24.216
Gilbert. "We can see all the beautiful houses where the rich people live.

46
00:04:24.217 --> 00:04:27.957
Nobody can live there unless he or she is a millionaire."

47
00:04:27.958 --> 00:04:34.759
"Oh, yes!" said Phil. "There's a perfectly gorgeous little place I want to show you, Anne.

48
00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:42.108
It wasn't built by a millionaire. I swear it must have grown out of the ground while Spofford Avenue was still a country road.

49
00:04:42.109 --> 00:04:47.507
I don't like the other houses there; they're too new and showy."

50
00:04:47.508 --> 00:04:50.863
They saw the small house as they walked up the hill.

51
00:04:50.864 --> 00:04:54.347
It was white, with pine trees on both sides.

52
00:04:54.348 --> 00:04:59.175
The whole place looked like it had been transplanted from some remote country village.

53
00:04:59.176 --> 00:05:04.090
"It's the sweetest place I've ever seen," said Anne, delighted.

54
00:05:04.091 --> 00:05:09.392
"It's even quainter than Miss Lavender's house."

55
00:05:09.393 --> 00:05:19.440
"Look at the name, Anne," said Phil, "in white letters around the archway over the gate: ‘Patty's Place.' Isn't that gorgeous?"

56
00:05:19.441 --> 00:05:23.430
"Have you any idea who Patty is?" asked Priscilla.

57
00:05:23.431 --> 00:05:32.206
"Patty Spofford is the name of the old lady who owns it, I've discovered," said Phil. "She lives there with her niece."

58
00:05:32.207 --> 00:05:36.816
"I'm going to dream about Patty's Place tonight," said Anne.

59
00:05:36.817 --> 00:05:42.068
"I wonder if, by any chance, we'll ever see the inside of it."

60
00:05:42.069 --> 00:05:45.119
"It isn't likely," said Priscilla.

61
00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:52.638
Anne smiled mysteriously. "No, it isn't likely, but I believe it will happen.

62
00:05:52.639 --> 00:06:04.254
I have a strange feeling that Patty's Place and I are going to become better acquainted."

